# Working conventions for this workspace — {DOMAIN} OS

You operate the {DOMAIN} OS. Your job is not just to file things — it is to keep the system **current,
compounding, and self-describing**, and to decide "done" and "consistent" by evidence (a
`{OS}-system-check-health` run), not by feeling. Act autonomously on the rules below. (AGENTS.md is a
symlink to this file, so editing this file updates both.)

## Managed work — preserve continuity without packaging everything

Before consequential work that another session or agent may continue, read `work/AGENTS.md`. Create a
managed package when work crosses a context boundary, has a blocker or review gate, carries
plan-changing uncertainty, requires durable evidence, or the user asks to track it. Search first and
resume equivalent work.

Routine same-turn work stays lightweight. A one-off deliverable may still use a dated
`work/{project}/YYYY-MM-DD-…/` activity folder without becoming a managed initiative.

The package owns coordination state. {ENTITY} records and {PIPELINE} stages own domain state. An
external system owns only fields declared in `operations/sync.md`. Link authorities; do not copy
their facts into competing records.

## Auto-manage loop (run on new input, without being asked)
1. Place the raw file in its correct home, dated YYYY-MM-DD (create the folder if it doesn't exist yet).
2. Read it.
3. Search managed packages and related {ENTITY}/{PIPELINE} records; resume existing work when relevant.
4. Extract only settled reusable value into `knowledge/` — keep raw findings with their package.
5. Reconcile the authoritative package, entity, pipeline, and declared external fields.
6. Update everything affected in the SAME turn: package state, entity READMEs, `{PIPELINE}/_board.md`,
   `artifacts/dashboard.html`, metrics. (never-stale rule)
7. Move domain folders to match reality; update only the external fields assigned to that system.
8. When evidence changes assumptions, acceptance, or sequencing, update the package and optional plan.
9. Mine for genuinely reusable templates, proof, FAQs, skills, checks, or evals.
10. Leave one exact next action and append a material lifecycle event to `PROGRESS.md`.

Confirm before inventing {DOMAIN} facts; otherwise just do the routine filing and summarize.

## Grow the structure on demand (don't pre-build; don't hoard folders)
The workspace starts as a lean spine (`plugin/ routines/ scripts/ docs/ work/` + root files). `work/`
always contains its human index and shared agent contract; optional package material exists only when
useful. Create another top-level folder the moment real material needs it — `knowledge/`, a pipeline,
an entity folder, or a brand-new one-word folder the domain genuinely calls for — and never create
folders speculatively. When you add or rename a folder, update this file's filing tree in the same turn.

## Self-updating brain (you MUST keep this file current)
This file is the brain; a stale brain is the worst drift there is. Whenever the system changes — a new
folder, a new skill, a new convention, a new routine, a moved boundary — update `CLAUDE.md` in the SAME
turn so it always matches reality. `AGENTS.md` is a symlink, so it follows automatically. The doctor
checks brain currency; don't make it catch a lie.

## Append material lifecycle events to PROGRESS.md
Append when an initiative starts, blocks, pauses, enters review, completes, cancels, or reopens; when
evidence changes direction; when durable value is promoted; or when OS machinery changes or is
verified:

```
## YYYY-MM-DD
- **{what you did}** — {why it was done, 1-2 sentences}.
```

Pitch it between technical and plain-English so a human and an agent both understand it. It is
history, not current state. Package READMEs hold current truth. Append; never rewrite history.

## Never stale (checked, not felt)
If you touched the project, leave it consistent — "consistent" is what `{OS}-system-check-health`
reports, not a feeling. After any change, ask which other files now disagree with reality and update
them all. `{OS}-system-fix-drift` (the janitor) runs the doctor + fixes drift on a schedule (its
runnable prompt is `routines/janitor.md`).

## Compound learning + self-improvement (only from evidence)
After a meaningful discovery or closure, evaluate whether anything is genuinely reusable. Capture it
with package provenance when yes; record a clean "nothing reusable" disposition when no. Search
`knowledge/` before solving a recurring question. When observed friction justifies improving the
machinery, run `{OS}-system-improve-machinery`; a grounded no-op is valid.

## After work, ask: "skillify this?"
When you finish a task, ask whether it will repeat, need upkeep, or prevent a failure from recurring.
If yes, run `{OS}-system-promote-skill` (skillify): it stages a draft under the source work package,
verifies it, then (with approval) publishes it and repackages. **Every installed skill lives in
`plugin/skills/`** and is named `{OS}-{area}-{verb}-{noun}` (4 words; maintenance organs use the area
`system`). Reusable scripts go in `scripts/`.

## Agent compatibility
`plugin/skills/` is the source of truth. `.agents/skills` points there so Cursor and Codex discover the
same skills. Don't edit `.agents/skills` directly, and don't create `.cursor/skills` unless asked.

## Filing decision tree
1. Consequential work that must survive context loss? -> follow `work/AGENTS.md`
2. Reusable instrument (template/checklist/boilerplate)? -> `library/`
3. Reusable executable script? -> `scripts/`
4. A task that will repeat or need upkeep? -> run `{OS}-system-promote-skill`
5. A finalized output you regenerate (dashboard, recurring report)? -> `artifacts/`
6. A simple one-off / ad-hoc build? -> `work/{project}/YYYY-MM-DD-…/`
7. Reusable answer/insight/decision/SOP? -> `knowledge/`
8. Human-facing doc about how this OS works? -> `docs/`
9. Flowing item not yet done? -> `{PIPELINE}/`
10. Tied to a core entity? -> `{ENTITY}/`
11. A workflow to run unattended? -> `routines/` (indexed in `routines/README.md`)
12. Something genuinely new the domain needs? -> a new one-word top-level folder, then update this tree.

## Don't
- Don't leave the system stale, the brain outdated, or a material lifecycle event unrecorded.
- Don't invent facts the user must own — mark inferred items "draft — confirm".
- Don't pre-create empty folders; don't author a skill outside `plugin/skills/`.
